/// An LRU cache with O(1) get and put.
///
/// The cache maintains a `HashMap` for O(1) lookups and a `LinkedList` to
/// track access order. The key insight for O(1) removal from the linked list
/// is that each node in the list stores a reference to its own position,
/// allowing us to remove it in O(1) without searching.
///
/// ## Ownership Issues
///
/// 1. **Self-referential structs**: The classic problem is that a `LinkedList`
///    node needs to know its own position so it can be removed in O(1). In
///    Rust, you can't easily have a struct that contains a reference to
///    itself (self-referential structs are not allowed). We work around this
///    by storing the key in the linked list node and using the key to look
///    up the node in the HashMap, but we still need a way to remove the node
